---
layout: article
title: Track custom events
description: Record the actions that matter to your product in Appwrite Analytics, such as signups and purchases, with event names and props from the browser, mobile apps, or your server.
---

Pageviews show where people go. Custom events show what they do there: a signup, a purchase, an invite sent, a plan upgraded. Each custom event has a name and optional props, and appears in the **Custom events** card on the property's dashboard.

# Send an event {% #send-an-event %}

Send a custom event with the `Tracking` class in your website or app, or from your server with an API key. These examples continue from the setup in [Track a website](/docs/products/analytics/web), [Track a mobile app](/docs/products/analytics/mobile), and [Track events from your server](/docs/products/analytics/server-side). With the [script tag](/docs/products/analytics/web#script-tag), call `track()` on `window.__appwriteTracker`.

{% multicode %}
```client-web
tracking.track('signup_completed', {
    props: { plan: 'pro', source: 'pricing_page' },
});
```
```client-flutter
tracking.event(
  'signup_completed',
  url: '/signup',
  props: {'plan': 'pro', 'source': 'pricing_page'},
);
```
```client-android-kotlin
tracking.event(
    "signup_completed",
    url = "/signup",
    props = mapOf("plan" to "pro", "source" to "pricing_page"),
)
```
```client-apple
tracking.event("signup_completed", url: "/signup", props: ["plan": "pro", "source": "pricing_page"])
```
```client-react-native
tracking.track('signup_completed', {
    url: '/signup',
    props: { plan: 'pro', source: 'pricing_page' },
});
```
```server-nodejs
await analytics.createEvent({
    propertyId: '<PROPERTY_ID>',
    name: 'signup_completed',
    url: 'https://example.com/signup',
    props: ['plan', 'pro', 'source', 'pricing_page'],
});
```
```server-deno
await analytics.createEvent({
    propertyId: '<PROPERTY_ID>',
    name: 'signup_completed',
    url: 'https://example.com/signup',
    props: ['plan', 'pro', 'source', 'pricing_page'],
});
```
```server-php
$analytics->createEvent(
    propertyId: '<PROPERTY_ID>',
    name: 'signup_completed',
    url: 'https://example.com/signup',
    props: ['plan', 'pro', 'source', 'pricing_page'],
);
```
```server-python
analytics.create_event(
    property_id='<PROPERTY_ID>',
    name='signup_completed',
    url='https://example.com/signup',
    props=['plan', 'pro', 'source', 'pricing_page'],
)
```
```server-ruby
analytics.create_event(
    property_id: '<PROPERTY_ID>',
    name: 'signup_completed',
    url: 'https://example.com/signup',
    props: ['plan', 'pro', 'source', 'pricing_page']
)
```
```server-dotnet
await analytics.CreateEvent(
    propertyId: "<PROPERTY_ID>",
    name: "signup_completed",
    url: "https://example.com/signup",
    props: new List<string> { "plan", "pro", "source", "pricing_page" }
);
```
```server-dart
await analytics.createEvent(
  propertyId: '<PROPERTY_ID>',
  name: 'signup_completed',
  url: 'https://example.com/signup',
  props: ['plan', 'pro', 'source', 'pricing_page'],
);
```
```server-kotlin
analytics.createEvent(
    propertyId = "<PROPERTY_ID>",
    name = "signup_completed",
    url = "https://example.com/signup",
    props = listOf("plan", "pro", "source", "pricing_page"),
)
```
```server-swift
_ = try await analytics.createEvent(
    propertyId: "<PROPERTY_ID>",
    name: "signup_completed",
    url: "https://example.com/signup",
    props: ["plan", "pro", "source", "pricing_page"]
)
```
```server-go
_, err := analytics.CreateEvent(
    "<PROPERTY_ID>",
    "signup_completed",
    "https://example.com/signup",
    analytics.WithCreateEventProps([]string{"plan", "pro", "source", "pricing_page"}),
)
if err != nil {
    log.Fatal(err)
}
```
```server-rust
analytics.create_event(
    "<PROPERTY_ID>",
    "signup_completed",
    "https://example.com/signup",
    None, // domain
    None, // referrer
    None, // screen_width
    None, // session_hash
    None, // scroll_depth
    None, // engagement_time
    Some(vec!["plan".into(), "pro".into(), "source".into(), "pricing_page".into()]),
    None, // user_id
    None, // ip
    None, // user_agent
    None, // interactive
).await?;
```
```bash
appwrite analytics create-event \
    --property-id '<PROPERTY_ID>' \
    --name signup_completed \
    --url https://example.com/signup \
    --props plan --props pro --props source --props pricing_page
```
```curl
curl -X POST 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1/analytics/properties/<PROPERTY_ID>/events' \
    -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
    -H 'X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>' \
    -d '{
        "name": "signup_completed",
        "url": "https://example.com/signup",
        "props": ["plan", "pro", "source", "pricing_page"]
    }'
```
{% /multicode %}

On the web, an event belongs to the current page unless the call passes a `url` option.

# Name your events {% #names %}

Event names are up to 64 characters. Pick names that describe a finished action, in lowercase `snake_case` with the object first and the verb second, such as `signup_completed`, `invite_sent`, or `plan_upgraded`. Keep names stable once you ship them: a renamed event starts a new row on the dashboard, and the old name keeps its history.

The `Tracking` class uses these names for its own events, so don't reuse them for yours:

- `pageview`
- `outbound_link`
- `file_download`
- `scroll_depth`
- `engagement_time`
- `screen_view`
- `first_open`
- `app_updated`
- `app_backgrounded`
- `app_foregrounded`
- `push_received`
- `push_opened`

An event named `pageview` counts as a real pageview, toward pageviews, bounce rate, and entry and exit pages, so use it only for [virtual pageviews](#virtual-pageviews).

A custom event counts as an interaction, so a visit with one isn't a [bounce](/docs/products/analytics/visitors#visit-metrics). For an event the visitor didn't cause, such as a heartbeat or a background sync, pass `interactive: false` so the event doesn't count as an interaction. It still extends the visit's duration, so a visit it keeps going for 10 seconds or more isn't a bounce.

# Add props {% #props %}

{% only_dark %}
![Event properties card with a tab for each prop key and the values of category](/images/docs/analytics/dark/event-props.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Event properties card with a tab for each prop key and the values of category](/images/docs/analytics/event-props.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Props add detail to an event, such as the plan someone chose or the button they clicked. Each event takes up to 16 props. Keys and values are text up to 255 characters each.

The `Tracking` class takes props as a map and turns each value into text. `createEvent` and REST take a flat list that alternates keys and values. A key without a value at the end of the list is dropped.

Appwrite stores props with the event. Don't put personal data such as email addresses or names in props.

# Track an AI agent {% #agents %}

{% only_dark %}
![Models and tools card on an Agent property, listing calls by tool](/images/docs/analytics/dark/agent-models-tools.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Models and tools card on an Agent property, listing calls by tool](/images/docs/analytics/agent-models-tools.avif)
{% /only_light %}

On an **Agent** property, send one event per model call and one per tool call. The **Models and tools** card counts them by the `model` and `tool` props:

| Event | Props |
| --- | --- |
| `model_called` | `model`, such as `claude-sonnet-5-5`. Optionally `provider`. |
| `tool_called` | `tool`, such as `web_search`. |

```server-nodejs
await analytics.createEvent({
    propertyId: '<PROPERTY_ID>',
    name: 'model_called',
    url: 'https://agent.example.com/run',
    props: ['model', 'claude-sonnet-5-5', 'provider', 'anthropic'],
    userId: '<USER_ID>',
});
```

The **Models and tools** card appears after the property receives an event with a `model` or `tool` prop. Click a model or tool to filter the whole dashboard to it. Props are stored as text, so the dashboard can't add up values such as token counts.

# Track a virtual pageview {% #virtual-pageviews %}

Some steps don't change the URL, such as the steps of a checkout form or a modal that works like a page. On the web, record them as pageviews with a URL of your own.

```client-web
tracking.pageview('https://example.com/checkout/payment');
```

Pass a full URL, including the scheme.

# Find events on the dashboard {% #dashboard %}

{% only_dark %}
![Custom events card listing add_to_cart, checkout_started, purchase_completed, and newsletter_signup](/images/docs/analytics/dark/custom-events.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Custom events card listing add_to_cart, checkout_started, purchase_completed, and newsletter_signup](/images/docs/analytics/custom-events.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The **Custom events** card lists every event name your app sends, except the automatic events above. Click an event to filter the whole dashboard to it: each point on the chart then counts the people and visits that triggered the event, and the **Pages**, **Locations**, and **Technology** cards break those events down by page, location, and device. The **Event properties** card ranks the values of each prop key, such as each `plan`, and clicking a value filters the dashboard the same way.

**Views per visit**, **Bounce rate**, **Visit duration**, and **Engagement time** describe whole visits, so they show `-` while an event filter is on, and so does **Users**. For more on filters, see [Filter and compare traffic](/docs/products/analytics/filters).
